For testing a new board, see: assembly_test/README

The general purpose firmware including Bluetooth functions is bluetooth_rxtx.

bl_update installs a bootloader over USB rather than running on the board; see
bl_update/README and ubertooth-dfu(1). It is built with `make bl_update`, not
by `make all`, because building it builds the bootloader, which needs the ARM
C++ runtime.

This firmware is intended to be compiled with a particular toolchain:

    https://launchpad.net/gcc-arm-embedded

If you are using a recent Ubuntu or Debian system, the toolchain can be installed
from their apt repositorities as follows:

    apt-get install gcc-arm-none-eabi

The default hardware target is Ubertooth One.  If you are compiling for
Ubertooth Zero, you must set the environment variable BOARD=UBERTOOTH_ZERO.


By default the bluetooth-rxtx firmware has transmit capabilities.  If you wish
to build firmware without the ability to transmit, you may do so by setting an
environment variable called DISABLE_TX.  For example:

    DISABLE_TX=1 make bluetooth_rxtx

The resulting firmware will still be named bluetooth_rxtx.bin.dfu but will be
receive only.  The build records the flags it compiled with, so turning
DISABLE_TX on or off rebuilds the objects; make clean is not needed first.

The board says which of the two it is running: this build calls itself
bluetooth_rx in its firmware info block, so ubertooth-util -v and
ubertooth-dfu name it that way, while the full build calls itself
bluetooth_rxtx.  Every host tool accepts either, since this is the same
firmware with its transmit half left out; a tool that transmits is refused by
the firmware, which answers no transmit request at all.

One exception, in a source release rather than a git checkout: the archive
ships one version.h per image directory, written for the full build, and a
build with no git history keeps the shipped header as it is.  So DISABLE_TX=1
in an unpacked release reports bluetooth_rxtx.  Delete
firmware/bluetooth_rxtx/version.h first if the name matters, which costs the
release number in the revision string.
